item.text:
Exposure: `limited_exposure_with_choices`. Scholz was Chancellor and coalition leader in 2022, but Germany had no national nuclear arsenal; the meaningful choices were alliance posture, procurement, arms-control diplomacy, and crisis safety policy. Under Article 65, the Chancellor set policy guidelines, while ministers ran portfolios independently, so this is not a direct nuclear-command case.

Reused evidence: none.

New evidence:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Lebenslauf Olaf Scholz","publisher":"Bundeskanzleramt","publication_date":"2026-07-20","url":"https://www.bundeskanzler.de/bk-de/content/kanzlerschaft-olaf-scholz-2344498","claim":"Olaf Scholz was elected in December 2021 as the ninth Federal Chancellor of Germany and led the first traffic-light coalition at federal level.","locator":"Biography section near the opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official federal biography page with a stable office chronology.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Establishes Scholz's office and inherited baseline for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct to Scholz; this is his official office record.","contrary_evidence":["None on office status."],"lenses":["1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Grundgesetz","publisher":"Deutscher Bundestag","publication_date":"2026-07-20","url":"https://www.bundestag.de/parlament/aufgaben/rechtsgrundlagen/grundgesetz/?id=gt125431","claim":"Article 63 says the Chancellor is elected by the Bundestag on the Federal President's proposal, and Article 65 says the Chancellor sets policy guidelines while ministers lead their departments independently; Article 65a gives the Defence Minister command authority in the defense portfolio.","locator":"Articles 63, 64, 65, and 65a","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary constitutional text from the Bundestag site, stable and authoritative.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly applicable in 2022 and defines Scholz's authority and constraints.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional constraint on Scholz's office, not personal conduct.","contrary_evidence":["Ministerial autonomy and coalition politics materially limit direct chancellor control."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Policy statement by Olaf Scholz, Chancellor of the Federal Republic of Germany and Member of the German Bundestag, 27 February 2022 in Berlin","publisher":"Federal Government","publication_date":"2022-02-27","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-en/service/archive/policy-statement-by-olaf-scholz-chancellor-of-the-federal-republic-of-germany-and-member-of-the-german-bundestag-27-february-2022-in-berlin-2008378","claim":"Scholz said that with regard to nuclear sharing Germany would procure a modern replacement for the outdated Tornado jets in good time, and that the F-35 could be used as a carrier aircraft.","locator":"Lines 358-362","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous statement by the Chancellor on the official federal website.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 decision point on Germany's nuclear-sharing posture.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Scholz statement and policy choice; cabinet-level commitment under his leadership.","contrary_evidence":["This preserved rather than reduced Germany's nuclear-sharing role.","Critics later argued it locked Germany into nuclear deterrence for years."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.5","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Policy statement by Olaf Scholz, Chancellor of the Federal Republic of Germany and Member of the German Bundestag, 27 February 2022 in Berlin","publisher":"Federal Government","publication_date":"2022-02-27","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-en/service/archive/policy-statement-by-olaf-scholz-chancellor-of-the-federal-republic-of-germany-and-member-of-the-german-bundestag-27-february-2022-in-berlin-2008378","claim":"Scholz said the government would strengthen resilience against cyberattacks and disinformation campaigns and against attacks on critical infrastructure and communication channels.","locator":"Lines 348-350","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous statement by the Chancellor on the official federal website.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 statement on a dual-use/catastrophic-risk adjacent domain.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Scholz policy framing; cabinet-level priority under his leadership.","contrary_evidence":["This is resilience rhetoric, not a demonstrated institutional reform or published cyber-risk doctrine.","It is indirect for nuclear/existential-risk scoring."],"lenses":["1B.7","1B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Federal Foreign Office on yesterday’s statement by the five permanent members of the UN Security Council","publisher":"Federal Foreign Office","publication_date":"2022-01-04","url":"https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/newsroom/news/2505084-2505084","claim":"The German Government welcomed the P5 statement that a nuclear war cannot be won and must never be fought, and said dialogue and cooperation among the five states were more important than ever for reducing tensions and escalation risk.","locator":"Quoted statement at lines 97-97","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official foreign ministry statement issued the day after the P5 declaration.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Very close to the 2022 period and directly relevant to nuclear rhetoric and de-escalation.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level position under Scholz's cabinet; not a private Scholz-only remark.","contrary_evidence":["It is a general government statement, not a personal command decision.","No direct implementation mechanism is specified."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.4","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"First Meeting of States Parties to the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"Federal Foreign Office","publication_date":"2022-06-20","url":"https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/aussenpolitik/themen/tpnw-wien-2538098","claim":"Germany participated as an observer in the first TPNW meeting, saying the government remains committed to a world without nuclear weapons, sees the NPT as the central framework, and is not joining the TPNW because of NATO responsibilities.","locator":"Lines 104-121","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official German foreign ministry explanation of the government's nuclear-disarmament posture.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Contemporaneous 2022 policy position.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet-level attribution under Scholz; the decision reflects government posture, not a personal order from a nuclear commander.","contrary_evidence":["The same text says NATO responsibilities keep Germany outside the TPNW.","The observer status is symbolic and does not itself reduce risk."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.6","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Non-Proliferation and Disarmament Initiative - 11th High-level Meeting Joint High-level Statement","publisher":"Federal Foreign Office","publication_date":"2022-08-01","url":"https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/newsroom/news/2546078-2546078","claim":"Germany and NPDI partners reaffirmed support for the NPT's full implementation, the ultimate goal of a world without nuclear weapons, and practical nuclear risk-reduction and disarmament steps.","locator":"Points 1, 2, 3, and 12","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official multilateral statement naming Germany as a member state and setting out specific nonproliferation aims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 NPT review-cycle evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level attribution, not Scholz personally, but under his chancellorship.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is multilateral and not specific to Scholz's personal initiative.","It reflects aspiration more than proved outcome."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.7","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement of the G7 Non-Proliferation Directors Group on a Nuclear Safety and Security Framework for Ukraine","publisher":"Federal Foreign Office","publication_date":"2022-03-15","url":"https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/newsroom/news/2517318-2517318","claim":"The G7 called for urgent measures to protect nuclear installations in Ukraine, emphasizing physical integrity, functional safety systems, secure power supply, staff able to make decisions free of undue pressure, and reliable communications with regulators.","locator":"Lines 102-117","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official G7 nonproliferation statement tied to the Ukraine war and nuclear safety conditions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 crisis period and directly relevant to nuclear accident prevention.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level German participation under Scholz's chancellorship; not a singular Scholz-only initiative.","contrary_evidence":["This concerns Ukraine's nuclear plants, not Germany's own command-and-control.","It is crisis mitigation, not a structural treaty breakthrough."],"lenses":["1B.8","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Speech by Olaf Scholz at the 77th general debate of the United Nations General Assembly","publisher":"Federal Government","publication_date":"2022-09-20","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-en/service/archive/speech-by-olaf-scholz-2128554","claim":"Scholz described Russia as a major nuclear power using violence to shift borders, condemned the war against Ukraine, and said Germany would support Ukraine with weapons and sanctions while standing by the UN order.","locator":"Lines 189-216 and 237-241","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct Scholz speech on the federal government's official archive page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Contemporaneous late-2022 crisis posture.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Scholz rhetoric and policy signaling; the support commitments reflect his government.","contrary_evidence":["This is general crisis diplomacy, not a direct nuclear safety or arms-control decision.","It accepts armed support to Ukraine, which can be read as escalatory in a broader security sense."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.8","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Germany to Buy up to 35 Lockheed F-35 Fighter Jets - Sources","publisher":"VOA News / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-03-14","url":"https://www.voanews.com/a/6483775.html","claim":"Reuters reported that Germany would purchase 35 F-35 fighters to replace the aging Tornado aircraft in the nuclear-sharing role, with the Tornado being the only German jet able to carry U.S. nuclear bombs.","locator":"Lines 61-103, especially 92-95","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting is strong independent contemporaneous coverage, even though the accessible host is VOA.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 procurement decision window.","ruler_attribution":"Indicates the government decision taken under Scholz's leadership; not a personal nuclear order.","contrary_evidence":["This is a continuation of nuclear-sharing capability rather than risk reduction.","The story notes France and procurement tradeoffs, showing broader strategic rather than safety logic."],"lenses":["1B.5","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"F-35: Why Germany is opting for the US stealth bomber","publisher":"DW","publication_date":"2022-03-16","url":"https://amp.dw.com/en/f-35-why-germany-is-opting-for-the-us-made-stealth-fighter-jet/a-61152127","claim":"DW reported that only the opposition Left Party clearly rejected the F-35 purchase, warning that nuclear sharing would fuel the danger of nuclear war in Europe, while an ECFR expert said the F-35 was better suited than older aircraft for delivering a nuclear weapon.","locator":"Lines 100-112","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent reporting with both expert explanation and documented domestic criticism.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 debate around the procurement choice.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant to Scholz's cabinet decision; the criticism targets the government choice rather than a separate actor.","contrary_evidence":["This is an analysis piece, not a primary decision record.","It contains a pro-F-35 expert view as well as anti-nuclear-sharing criticism."],"lenses":["1B.5","1B.6","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Linke fragt Beschaffung des Kampfflugzeuges F-35","publisher":"Deutscher Bundestag","publication_date":"2022-05-18","url":"https://www.bundestag.de/presse/hib/kurzmeldungen-896118","claim":"The Left parliamentary group asked whether buying the F-35 would commit Germany to nuclear sharing for decades and noted earlier government concerns about technical risks and a poorer cost-benefit ratio.","locator":"Lines 734-736","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary parliamentary record of contemporaneous criticism and the government's earlier risk concerns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 domestic accountability debate.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but material to Scholz's government decision; it captures legislative scrutiny of the cabinet's choice.","contrary_evidence":["This is opposition framing, not an executive defense or neutral technical assessment.","It does not prove the final operational impact of the purchase."],"lenses":["1B.6","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Jahresabrüstungsbericht 2022","publisher":"Auswärtiges Amt","publication_date":"2023-04-26","url":"https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/de/aussenpolitik/sicherheitspolitik/abruestung-ruestungskontrolle/jahresabruestungsbericht-2022-2594298","claim":"The German government summarized 2022 as a year in which it took responsibility for arms control, disarmament, and nonproliferation under difficult conditions, including proposals for risk reduction and disarmament verification, observer participation at the TPNW meeting, and G7 work on Ukrainian nuclear safety.","locator":"Lines 163-169","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official retrospective government summary of 2022 actions; useful corroboration but not contemporaneous.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Retrospective summary of 2022 policy rather than direct contemporaneous evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet-level attribution under Scholz; indirect but relevant to end-of-year posture.","contrary_evidence":["Published in 2023, so it is retrospective and may smooth over internal disagreement.","It summarizes government action rather than Scholz's personal decisions."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.7","1B.8","1B.9","1B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary:
- I considered roughly 25-40 documents across official German pages, Bundestag records, UN/CTBTO/IAEA material, and independent reporting.
- Kept the strongest non-duplicative URLs: federal chancellery biography, Bundestag Basic Law, two Scholz government speeches, three Foreign Office nonproliferation pages, one G7 nuclear-safety statement, one Reuters wire story, one DW analysis, and one Bundestag inquiry record.
- Rejected duplicate mirrors and translations of the same government pages where the underlying claim was already captured once.
- Rejected several broad security and energy pages because they were too indirect for 1B or because they concerned nuclear power rather than nuclear/existential-risk authority.
- Rejected late 2025/2026 pages and other out-of-period material when they did not add period-specific 2022 value.
- Rejected syndicated duplicates of the Reuters F-35 story that added no new claim beyond the VOA-hosted Reuters copy.
- Rejected purely contextual pages on Germany's general security policy when they did not bear on Scholz's 2022 nuclear-sharing, arms-control, or crisis-safety choices.

Remaining gaps:
- There is no public evidence that Scholz personally handled nuclear command-and-control, because Germany had no national nuclear arsenal.
- Public sources do not show private crisis calls or internal coordination details with nuclear-weapon states; the record is mostly formal speeches and policy statements.
- Evidence on 1B.3 is thin beyond procurement and alliance-custody context; there is no German sovereign nuclear custody regime to inspect.
- 1B.9 is mostly indirect: cyber, biosafety, and space-risk language exists, but I did not find a strong Scholz-specific existential-risk reform record for 2022.
- More search would probably help only marginally unless you want a deeper file on nuclear-sharing procurement or on Germany's Ukraine nuclear-safety diplomacy.
